/P 166 0 R
/E (CSV-to-database mapping: the station value is matched to stations.station_name; its station_id is then placed in the reading row. The CSV date and time become reading_time. PM2.5 is converted to a numeric REAL value only after validation. Optional sensor columns are stored when available. A unique station/time rule prevents the same station reading from being inserted twice. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [37]
>>
endobj
318 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 166 0 R
/E (If the system kept only CSV files and loaded them into pandas for every question, pandas would read the file into a DataFrame, filter rows and perform groupby or aggregate operations. A full scan generally takes O\(n\) time for n readings and the DataFrame needs O\(n\) memory. The data remains on disk as CSV, but the working copy occupies memory each time it is loaded. SQLite can use an index to locate matching records in approximately O\(log n\) search time, plus the time needed to return the matching rows. A query that calculates a statistic over every row still has to process those rows. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [38]
>>
endobj
319 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 166 0 R
/E (Conclusion: use CSV as the interchange/input format and SQLite as the main queryable store. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [39]
>>
endobj
