437 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 171 0 R
/E (A Python loop can read n rows once and maintain a dictionary keyed by \(station, date\). For each reading it updates the group's minimum, maximum, running sum and count. This takes O\(n\) time. The dictionary stores one summary per station-day group, so memory is O\(g\), where g is the number of groups, rather than storing all n readings. SQL GROUP BY also processes the input rows and maintains group state inside the database. Both are generally linear for a full aggregation, although indexing, sorting and database overhead can affect actual timings. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
